<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/advanced/exoplanets-and-detection">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    In 1991 the textbook answer was: <strong>we have no evidence for planets outside the solar
    system</strong>. Today the count exceeds 5,800 confirmed worlds — hot Jupiters, super-Earths,
    mini-Neptunes, systems with six planets in resonant clockwork. Most were found not by seeing
    them but by <strong>watching their stars for dips and wobbles</strong> — astronomy's
    indirect-detection masterpiece. The syllabus unit packages the same journey:
    how planetary systems form (12.1–12.3), the methods that discover them (12.4), the
    habitable zones where life might sit (12.5–12.6), and the Drake-equation framework
    (12.7–12.8) that turns "are we alone?" into a chain of estimable factors. This page
    covers all four, with the quantitative core — how a dip in starlight becomes a
    planet's radius, mass, orbit and atmosphere — done properly.
  </p>
  <p>
    History in three dates. <strong>1992</strong>: the first confirmed exoplanets — two
    terrestrial-mass bodies orbiting a <em>pulsar</em> (Wolszczan &amp; Frail), found by
    timing the dead star's ticks to millisecond precision. A weird first, around a
    stellar corpse, but unambiguous. <strong>1995</strong>: 51 Pegasi b — a
    Jupiter-mass planet on a 4.2-day orbit around a sun-like star (Mayor &amp; Queloz;
    Nobel 2019), found by radial velocity; "hot Jupiters" were so unexpected that
    theorists had to invent planetary <em>migration</em> to explain them — giant
    planets forming far out and spiralling inward through the natal disk. From 2009
    (Kepler), <strong>transits</strong> multiplied the census by thousands; from 2022
    (JWST), <strong>atmospheres</strong> became readable. Each decade moved one more
    property of these worlds from speculation to measurement.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="how a dot of starlight betrays an invisible world" widget="equationStep" config={transitCfg} />

  <h2>The transit method, quantitatively</h2>
  <p>
    A transit is a miniature eclipse: the planet crosses the stellar disk and blocks a
    flux fraction δ = (R<sub>p</sub>/R<sub>s</sub>)². The arithmetic chain that turns
    one dimming into a world:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the transit-to-planet pipeline — depth, period, wobble, density, atmosphere" widget="equationStep" config={calcCfg} />

  <p>
    The bias structure matters as much as the sensitivity: only orbits nearly edge-on
    transit (probability ≈ R<sub>s</sub>/a — about 0.5% for an Earth twin), and close-in
    planets transit more often with deeper dips, so transits over-represent hot, large
    worlds. Correcting for that geometry is how Kepler converted ~4,800 confirmed
    planets (plus thousands of candidates) into honest occurrence <em>rates</em>: most
    stars host planets; the commonest planet type in the galaxy is neither Earth nor
    Jupiter but the <strong>super-Earth/mini-Neptune</strong> class (1–4 Earth radii),
    absent from our own system; and the "radius valley" — a scarcity of planets at
    ~1.8 Earth radii — marks the boundary where planets keep or lose their hydrogen
    envelopes to photoevaporation. Even the architecture was a surprise: many systems
    pack 4–7 sub-Neptune planets into tighter spaces than Mercury's orbit, several in
    <strong>resonant chains</strong> (TRAPPIST-1's seven Earth-sized worlds, 40
    light-years away, cycle in a 3:2, 3:2, 2:1... clockwork whose transit-timing
    variations — planets arriving early or late because siblings tug them — also
    revealed their masses).
  </p>

  <h2>The wobble and its siblings: the other five methods</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Radial velocity</strong> watches the star, not the planet: a planet of mass
    M<sub>p</sub> on orbit radius a drags its star in a mirror orbit with speed
    v = 30 km/s × (M<sub>p</sub>/M<sub>s</sub>) × (1 AU/a)^½, read as a periodic
    Doppler shift of the star's spectral lines. Modern spectrographs (HARPS, ESPRESSO)
    reach ~30 cm/s precision — enough for super-Earths, still a factor ~30 short of
    the Sun's 9 cm/s response to Earth, which is why true Earth twins remain RV's
    frontier. The method's historical weight is unmatched: 51 Peg b's 57 m/s signal,
    arriving in perfect 4.2-day rhythm, was the doorway. <strong>Direct imaging</strong>
    is what the name promises — blocking the star's light with a coronagraph and
    photographing the planet — feasible only for young, hot, self-luminous giants on
    wide orbits (the four-planet HR 8799 system; Beta Pictoris b), but the method that
    future flagship telescopes will aim at Earth twins. <strong>Microlensing</strong>
    exploits Einstein gravity: a foreground star passing exactly in front of a
    background one briefly lenses it, and a planet makes a sharp blip on the light
    curve — the method that finds planets at thousands of light-years and even
    <strong>free-floating worlds</strong> unbound to any star (more of these are being
    found than expected). <strong>Astrometry</strong> measures the star's positional
    wobble on the sky — Gaia is delivering it at microarcsecond precision for giant
    planets. <strong>Pulsar timing</strong> found the first. Each method samples
    different masses and orbits; the full picture only exists because they overlap.
  </p>

  <h2>Habitable zones — and why the phrase needs unpacking (12.5–12.6)</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>circumstellar habitable zone</strong> is the orbital band where a
    planet with an Earth-like atmosphere can hold liquid surface water: bounded inside
    by a runaway greenhouse (oceans boil into a Venus — the inner edge sits just
    inside Earth's orbit at ~0.95 AU for the Sun) and outside by maximum CO₂
    greenhouse (the outer edge near ~1.7 AU — nearly Mars's orbit). The zone scales
    with stellar luminosity as √L, so it hugs red dwarfs tightly: TRAPPIST-1's
    three inner planets sit closer to their star than Mercury does to the Sun. Two
    caveats keep the concept honest. <strong>The zone is necessary, not
    sufficient</strong>: atmosphere, water inventory, magnetic field and geological
    cycling decide the rest — Venus and Mars both sit near the Sun's HZ edges and
    both lost their shot. And <strong>M-dwarf habitability has open problems</strong>:
    tidally locked worlds may or may not retain atmospheres, and young red dwarfs
    flare violently, potentially sterilising or stripping their planets — TRAPPIST-1
    worlds' atmospheres are a live observational question, not an assumed yes.
    Estimates from Kepler's statistics put roughly one in five sun-like stars with an
    Earth-sized planet in its habitable zone — on the order of ten billion such
    worlds in the Milky Way, and tens of billions counting red dwarfs.
  </p>

  <h2>Atmospheres and the biosignature prize</h2>
  <p>
    During a transit, starlight skims through the planet's atmosphere, and each
    molecule absorbs its own colours — the planet appears slightly larger in some
    wavelengths than others, and the difference is a <strong>transmission
    spectrum</strong>. The signal scales with the atmospheric scale height
    H = kT/(mg) — puffy hot atmospheres are easier to read — which is why JWST's first
    targets were hot Jupiters: WASP-39b's spectrum in 2022 gave the first unambiguous
    exoplanet <strong>CO₂</strong>, plus water and sulfur chemistry. The 2023 report
    of a possible dimethyl-sulfide feature on the sub-Neptune K2-18b shows both the
    promise and the discipline required: the candidate feature sits at the edge of
    detectability, competing models explain it without life, and the community's
    response — replicate with more transits, test abiotic pathways — is how such
    claims should be handled. The agreed <strong>biosignature</strong> gold standard is
    chemical disequilibrium: oxygen plus methane together in one atmosphere (each
    destroys the other, so something must replenish both — on Earth, life). The
    instruments that can actually do this for true Earth twins are the 2030s–40s
    generation: 30-metre ground telescopes and NASA's proposed Habitable Worlds
    Observatory.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> exoplanet statistics mean "Earth 2.0 is around the
    corner, habitable and inhabited." The measurements so far establish <em>size and
    insolation</em> — necessary conditions. Whether those worlds have atmospheres,
    water, magnetic protection or chemistry is a separate, harder measurement we are
    only beginning. Honesty here is what keeps the field credible.
  </p>

  <h2>A field guide to the landmark worlds</h2>
  <p>
    Naming names keeps the statistics concrete. <strong>51 Pegasi b</strong> (1995): the
    hot Jupiter that started it, half Jupiter's mass on a 4.2-day orbit. <strong>Proxima
    Centauri b</strong> (2016): the nearest exoplanet, 4.2 light-years away, roughly
    Earth-massed in its red dwarf's habitable zone — and blasted by flares, the M-dwarf
    problem in one address. <strong>Kepler-186f</strong> (2014): the first
    Earth-<em>radius</em> planet confirmed in a habitable zone. <strong>TRAPPIST-1's
    seven</strong> (2017): the resonant system, all Earth-sized, three in the zone,
    masses nailed by transit-timing variations — the community's most-observed system
    and JWST's prime atmosphere target. <strong>HR 8799's four giants</strong> (2008):
    the first directly imaged system, their orbits visibly moving between exposures.
    <strong>K2-18b</strong> (2015): the sub-Neptune carrying the water-vapour and
    contested-biosignature headlines. Each earned its fame by breaking an expectation —
    and the census's collective message is that no single architecture deserves to be
    called normal.
  </p>

  <h2>Second-order signals: what else a transit can say</h2>
  <p>
    The full transit light curve carries more than depth. The <strong>duration</strong>
    (a few hours, scaling with the orbital period and impact parameter) and the
    rounded shape of <strong>ingress and egress</strong> pin down the orbit's tilt and
    the planet's speed; repeated transits give the period to one part in a million
    over years — precision that reveals additional planets through timing perturbations
    before they are ever seen. When the planet passes <em>behind</em> the star (the
    <strong>secondary eclipse</strong>), the blocked light is the planet's own thermal
    glow: subtracting it measures a <strong>temperature</strong> — hot-Jupiter
    day-sides read at 1,500–2,500 K, and wind patterns inferred from where the hottest
    point sits east of the substellar spot. The <strong>phase curve</strong> —
    brightness through the whole orbit as the planet shows day, then night — maps
    clouds and heat transport on worlds no camera will ever resolve. And
    <strong>ellipsoidal variations</strong> (the star tugged slightly egg-shaped)
    independently constrain the planet's mass from photometry alone. One star, one
    photometer, a dozen independent measurements — this is why transit surveys
    multiplied faster than every other technique combined.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Formation connects to the census (12.1–12.3).</strong> Planets grow in
    disks: dust grains collide into pebbles, pebbles drift inward and accrete into
    planetesimals, and cores either gather gas fast (becoming giants before the disk
    disperses, in a few million years) or stay rocky. The exoplanet census is the
    theory's test bench: the radius valley is the photoevaporation boundary read
    directly; hot Jupiters forced migration theory into the mainstream; resonance
    chains record disks that stopped migrating just in time. The solar system's
    oddity — no super-Earth, everything well-spaced — may owe to Jupiter's history,
    including the postulated Grand Tack migration that truncated Mars's food supply.
    Formation, in other words, is no longer a separate chapter: it is the explanation
    of the statistics.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The Drake equation and SETI (12.7–12.8).</strong> Frank Drake's 1961
    framing, N = R<sub>★</sub>·f<sub>p</sub>·n<sub>e</sub>·f<sub>l</sub>·f<sub>i</sub>·f<sub>c</sub>·L,
    multiplies star-formation rate, planet fraction, habitable worlds, life
    probability, intelligence, communication, and lifetime of detectability. Its real
    function is diagnostic: the first three factors are now <em>measured</em>
    (stars form; planets are common; rocky HZ worlds number in the billions), so the
    uncertainty has migrated into biology — how often life starts, how often it
    becomes technological, and how long it lasts (the L term, darkly measurable from
    our own trajectory). The <strong>Fermi paradox</strong> — where is everybody, given
    the numbers? — is the equation's empirical shadow; proposed resolutions range from
    rarity of intelligence to the "great filter" behind us or ahead of us. SETI
    itself continues: Breakthrough Listen has scanned over a million nearby stars with
    no confirmed artificial signal; the 1977 "Wow!" signal remains unexplained and
    unrepeated. The exoplanet revolution has, at minimum, changed the equation's
    first terms from guesses into data.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> The M-dwarf atmosphere question (do TRAPPIST-1
    worlds have any?) is the near-term decider for habitable-zone realism. The
    runaway-greenhouse boundary is uncertain enough that Venus's history — was it ever
    habitable? — is undecided, with a DAVINCI-style descent probe the proposed
    experiment. Direct imaging of Earth twins demands contrast of 10⁻¹⁰ at
    milliarcsecond separations, the hardest observation ever attempted. And the
    statistical endgame — <em>η</em><sub>Earth</sub>, the exact frequency of Earth
    analogues — awaits PLATO and the Rubin Observatory's transit hauls. The page's
    technique section is the toolkit; these are the tool's unfinished jobs.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Mayor &amp; Queloz (1995, Nobel 2019); NASA Exoplanet Archive; Petigura, Howard &amp; Marcy (2013) on Earth-analogue occurrence; JWST results (2022–); JWST WASP-39b transmission spectrum (2022).</p>
